\title{Time Travel Consistency:\\Novikov's Principle as a Fixed-Point Theorem}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 19, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A closed causal circuit turns an initial-value description into a boundary-value problem: the state returning from one circuit must equal the state entering it. We formulate this requirement as a fixed-point equation and identify a precise sufficient mechanism for Novikov self-consistency. If the one-circuit return map is a contraction on a nonempty complete metric state space, a unique self-consistent state exists, every iterated traversal converges to it, and the one-step consistency defect gives an explicit a posteriori error bound. We specialize the result to real polynomial maps on complete invariant domains and to affine feedback, where the consistent state is explicit. A finite-state analogue is also established. The counterexamples $x\mapsto x^2+1$ over the reals and Boolean negation show that neither polynomiality nor the mere existence of a causal rule guarantees consistency. The framework separates algebraic solvability, dynamical stability, and physical domain invariance while interpreting causal consistency as stable feedback.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Cut a closed causal circuit at a chosen hypersurface and record the state $x$ crossing the cut. Let $F$ summarize every interaction encountered during one complete round trip. Boundary data close consistently exactly when
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fixed}
F(x)=x.
\end{equation}
The consistency question is therefore a fixed-point problem. This reformulation is elementary, but it exposes the assumptions needed for a genuine existence theorem. A general self-map may have no fixed point, one fixed point, or many; even polynomial self-maps of the real line need not have fixed points.

The structural condition studied here is strict contraction. If one traversal reduces every pairwise discrepancy by a common factor $K<1$, repeated traversal erases dependence on the initial boundary proposal. Completeness retains the limiting state inside the model. The same geometry supplies uniqueness, global attraction, and an operational error estimate.

The conclusions are conditional. They do not establish the physical existence of closed timelike curves or derive a return map from spacetime field equations. They state that once a causal circuit is represented by a nonempty complete state space and a contractive one-circuit map, the associated boundary-value problem is uniquely and stably self-consistent.

\section{Causal boundary-value problems}

\begin{definition}[Causal boundary-value problem]
Let $X$ be a set of admissible boundary states. A causal boundary-value problem on $X$ consists of a return map $F:X\to X$, where $F(x)$ is the boundary state obtained after $x$ completes one causal circuit.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Self-consistency]
A state $x\in X$ is self-consistent if $F(x)=x$. The problem is Novikov-consistent if at least one self-consistent state exists.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Fixed-point characterization]\label{prop:char}
A causal boundary-value problem is Novikov-consistent if and only if its return map has a fixed point.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By definition, consistency means that some boundary state returns unchanged. That statement is precisely the fixed-point equation~\eqref{eq:fixed}.
\end{proof}

Suppose now that $X$ carries a metric $d$. A return map is a \emph{contraction with factor $K$} if $0\leq K<1$ and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:contract}
d(F(x),F(y))\leq Kd(x,y)
\end{equation}
for all $x,y\in X$. Induction gives
\[
d(F^n(x),F^n(y))\leq K^n d(x,y).
\]
A metric space is complete when every Cauchy sequence converges to a point of the space. In the present interpretation, completeness prevents increasingly compatible histories from approaching a missing, inadmissible boundary state.

\section{The Novikov--Banach theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Existence and uniqueness under contraction]\label{thm:main}
Let $(X,d)$ be a nonempty complete metric space, and let $F:X\to X$ satisfy~\eqref{eq:contract} for some $0\leq K<1$. Then there is exactly one $x_\ast\in X$ such that $F(x_\ast)=x_\ast$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose $x_0\in X$ and set $x_{n+1}=F(x_n)$. Contraction gives
\[
d(x_{n+1},x_n)\leq K^n d(x_1,x_0).
\]
For $m>n$, the triangle inequality and the geometric-series formula imply
\[
d(x_m,x_n)
 \leq \sum_{j=n}^{m-1}d(x_{j+1},x_j)
 \leq \frac{K^n}{1-K}d(x_1,x_0).
\]
Hence $(x_n)$ is Cauchy. Completeness provides a limit $x_\ast\in X$. A contraction is Lipschitz continuous, so
\[
F(x_\ast)=F\left(\lim_{n\to\infty}x_n\right)
 =\lim_{n\to\infty}x_{n+1}=x_\ast.
\]
If $x_\ast$ and $y_\ast$ were both fixed, then
\[
d(x_\ast,y_\ast)
=d(F(x_\ast),F(y_\ast))
\leq Kd(x_\ast,y_\ast).
\]
Since $K<1$, their distance must be zero. Thus the fixed point is unique.
\end{proof}

The proof constructs rather than merely asserts the consistent state. It also gives the following dynamical result.

\begin{theorem}[Global attraction]\label{thm:attraction}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, every initial state $x_0\in X$ satisfies
\[
\lim_{n\to\infty}F^n(x_0)=x_\ast.
\]
More precisely,
\[
d(F^n(x_0),x_\ast)\leq K^n d(x_0,x_\ast).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Because $F^n(x_\ast)=x_\ast$, apply the contraction inequality $n$ times to the pair $(x_0,x_\ast)$. The right-hand side tends to zero.
\end{proof}

Thus consistency is not an isolated algebraic coincidence. The unique consistent state globally attracts every repeatedly traversed boundary proposal.

\subsection{A posteriori certification}

For a proposed state $x$, define its \emph{consistency defect} by $r(x)=d(x,F(x))$. It compares the entering and returning data after one circuit.

\begin{theorem}[A posteriori consistency error]\label{thm:residual}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, every $x\in X$ obeys
\begin{equation}\label{eq:residual}
d(x,x_\ast)\leq \frac{d(x,F(x))}{1-K}.
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Using the triangle inequality, the fixed-point equation, and contraction,
\begin{align*}
d(x,x_\ast)
&\leq d(x,F(x))+d(F(x),x_\ast)\\
&=d(x,F(x))+d(F(x),F(x_\ast))\\
&\leq d(x,F(x))+Kd(x,x_\ast).
\end{align*}
Rearrangement gives~\eqref{eq:residual}.
\end{proof}

The estimate makes consistency quantitatively testable. A measured one-circuit mismatch at most $\varepsilon$ certifies distance at most $\varepsilon/(1-K)$ from the exact consistent state. Near $K=1$, weak damping makes the problem poorly conditioned; when $K$ is small, the same residual is a strong certificate.

Combining the estimate with iteration also yields
\[
d(F^n(x_0),x_\ast)
\leq \frac{K^n}{1-K}d(x_0,F(x_0)),
\]
which supports a computable stopping rule without prior knowledge of $x_\ast$.

\section{Polynomial causal maps}

Let $p\in\R[t]$ and let $S\subseteq\R$ be the physically admissible state domain. The domain is \emph{invariant} when $p(S)\subseteq S$. Invariance is essential: without it, one traversal may eject an admissible state from the model. Completeness ensures that limits of admissible Cauchy sequences remain admissible.

\begin{theorem}[Polynomial consistency on an invariant domain]\label{thm:poly}
Let $p$ be a real polynomial and let $S\subseteq\R$ be nonempty and complete under the Euclidean metric. Suppose $p(S)\subseteq S$ and, for some $0\leq K<1$,
\[
|p(x)-p(y)|\leq K|x-y|
\]
for all $x,y\in S$. Then there is exactly one $x_\ast\in S$ satisfying $p(x_\ast)=x_\ast$. Every iteration $x_{n+1}=p(x_n)$ begun in $S$ converges to $x_\ast$, and every $x\in S$ satisfies
\[
|x-x_\ast|\leq \frac{|x-p(x)|}{1-K}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Invariance makes the restriction of $p$ a self-map of $S$. The assumed estimate makes that restriction a contraction. Apply Theorems~\ref{thm:main}, \ref{thm:attraction}, and~\ref{thm:residual}.
\end{proof}

For a closed interval $S=[L,U]$, a practical sufficient test is
\[
p([L,U])\subseteq[L,U],
\qquad
\sup_{x\in[L,U]}|p'(x)|\leq K<1.
\]
The mean value theorem then supplies the contraction inequality. Polynomiality itself is not the source of consistency; it is a modeling class in which invariance and contraction can often be analyzed algebraically.

\subsection{Affine feedback}

\begin{theorem}[Explicit affine consistency]\label{thm:affine}
Let $F(x)=ax+b$ for $a,b\in\R$. If $|a|<1$, then $F$ has exactly one fixed point,
\[
x_\ast=\frac{b}{1-a}.
\]
For every $x_0\in\R$,
\[
F^n(x_0)=x_\ast+a^n(x_0-x_\ast),
\]
so the orbit converges geometrically to $x_\ast$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The identity $|F(x)-F(y)|=|a|\,|x-y|$ makes $F$ a contraction. Solving $ax+b=x$ gives the displayed fixed point; $1-a\neq0$ follows from $|a|<1$. Subtraction of the fixed-point equation from $x_{n+1}=ax_n+b$ yields $x_{n+1}-x_\ast=a(x_n-x_\ast)$, and induction gives the iteration formula.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
For $F(x)=x/2+3$, the unique consistent state is $6$. Starting from $0$ gives
\[
0,\ 3,\ 4.5,\ 5.25,\ 5.625,\ldots,
\]
and starting from $20$ gives
\[
20,\ 13,\ 9.5,\ 7.75,\ 6.875,\ldots.
\]
At $x=5.625$, the residual is $0.1875$. Formula~\eqref{eq:residual} gives
\[
|x-6|\leq\frac{0.1875}{1-0.5}=0.375,
\]
which is exact here.
\end{example}

\section{Finite state spaces}

\begin{theorem}[Finite strict-contraction consistency]\label{thm:finite}
Let $(X,d)$ be a nonempty finite metric space. If $F:X\to X$ satisfies
\[
d(F(x),F(y))\leq Kd(x,y)
\]
for all $x,y\in X$ and some $K<1$, then $F$ has exactly one fixed point.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every orbit in a finite set eventually repeats and enters a periodic cycle. A cycle of length greater than one contains a pair of distinct points whose positive distance returns to itself after a full period, while repeated strict contraction would make that distance smaller. Hence the eventual cycle has length one and supplies a fixed point. Two distinct fixed points are excluded by the same distance argument.
\end{proof}

This is an elementary finite counterpart to Theorem~\ref{thm:main}. Strict contraction eliminates every nontrivial recurrent pattern.

\section{Counterexamples and boundaries}

\begin{proposition}[Quadratic inconsistency]\label{prop:quadratic}
The real polynomial return map $F(x)=x^2+1$ has no self-consistent state.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
A fixed point would satisfy
\[
x^2-x+1=\left(x-\frac12\right)^2+\frac34=0,
\]
but the left-hand side is strictly positive for every real $x$.
\end{proof}

Thus polynomiality alone does not imply consistency. In particular, no nonempty complete invariant domain can exist on which this map is a strict contraction, because Theorem~\ref{thm:poly} would then produce a fixed point.

\begin{proposition}[Boolean grandfather paradox]\label{prop:boolean}
On $X=\{\mathrm{false},\mathrm{true}\}$, the return map $F(b)=\neg b$ has no fixed point.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Negation sends false to true and true to false, so neither state returns unchanged.
\end{proof}

Under the discrete metric, negation preserves the distance between the two states. Its Lipschitz factor is $1$, not a number below $1$, and its orbit is a two-cycle. This places it exactly beyond the strict-contraction hypothesis.

The examples isolate the role of each assumption. A deterministic causal rule need not be consistent; a polynomial rule need not have a real fixed point; invariance is needed to define repeated evolution on a proposed domain; completeness retains limiting states; and strict contraction supplies uniqueness and global attraction. Strict contraction is sufficient, not necessary. The identity map has fixed points with factor $1$, and maps that fail to contract globally may still possess locally attracting fixed points.

\section{Algorithms and numerical certification}

A direct computational method is fixed-point iteration:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item Choose $x_0$ in the invariant domain.
\item Compute $x_{n+1}=F(x_n)$.
\item Compute $e_{n+1}=d(x_{n+1},F(x_{n+1}))/(1-K)$.
\item Stop when $e_{n+1}\leq\varepsilon$.
\end{enumerate}
Theorem~\ref{thm:residual} certifies that the returned state lies within $\varepsilon$ of $x_\ast$. Each step uses one or two evaluations of $F$ depending on whether values are reused, one distance computation, and constant state storage. For evaluation cost $C_F$, $n$ iterations cost $O(nC_F)$ apart from metric overhead.

For $0<K<1$, the initial residual provides an a priori iteration estimate through
\[
\frac{K^n}{1-K}d(x_0,F(x_0))\leq\varepsilon.
\]
The residual stopping rule is generally preferable because it adapts to the observed orbit.

For a polynomial on $[L,U]$, a sufficient-condition pipeline first verifies $p([L,U])\subseteq[L,U]$, then bounds $|p'|$ by $K<1$, and finally iterates with the residual certificate. Polynomial critical points, interval arithmetic, or certified optimization can establish the first two steps. Sampling is useful for illustration but cannot by itself certify a global inequality.

For a finite table-defined map, scanning every state for $F(x)=x$ costs $O(|X|)$ map evaluations. Orbit tracing reveals nontrivial cycles such as Boolean negation. Under a strict-contraction certificate, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite} guarantees exactly one fixed point.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

The return-map abstraction is analogous to a Poincar\'e map. It compresses local evolution around a circuit into a map across one chosen cut. In a detailed spacetime model, constructing $F$ would require solving the local evolution equations; the present results apply once the induced map and its metric properties are known.

The same framework describes ordinary feedback. A closed-loop controller maps one cycle's error to the next. Contraction gives a unique equilibrium and suppresses perturbations. In recurrent computation and equilibrium networks, a representation is likewise defined by $F(x)=x$; contractivity prevents incompatible equilibria and makes naive iteration reliable.

The residual estimate is particularly significant. Exact equality can appear fragile, but contraction makes approximate consistency robust: a small observable mismatch guarantees a nearby exact solution. The amplification factor $(1-K)^{-1}$ acts as a condition number. Strongly contractive loops are easy to certify; nearly neutral loops demand finer measurements.

The framework also has limits. It assumes deterministic one-circuit evolution and depends on the chosen metric. Global contraction may be stronger than physical stability in one basin. No abstract return map replaces the spacetime dynamics from which it should arise. The theorem is best read as an interface: detailed physics supplies a state space and return law; fixed-point geometry supplies consistency consequences.

\section{Future directions}

Local contraction is the most immediate extension. A polynomial preserving a closed interval may have a fixed point with derivative magnitude below one even when no global contraction bound holds. Its immediate basin should still select a locally unique consistent history, with critical and repelling periodic points organizing basin boundaries.

One-parameter polynomial families invite a bifurcation classification. The number of fixed points changes where $p(x)-x$ acquires a multiple real root, while dynamical stability changes where $|p'(x)|=1$ at a fixed point. These algebraic and dynamical loci need not coincide.

A perturbation theorem should compare laws as well as states. If $F$ and $G$ share contraction factor $K<1$ and have fixed points $x_F,x_G$, the natural estimate is
\[
d(x_F,x_G)\leq
\frac{\sup_x d(F(x),G(x))}{1-K}.
\]
It would convert uncertainty in the return law into uncertainty in the selected history.

For stochastic loops, a boundary state can be replaced by a probability law and the return map by a Markov operator. Contraction in Wasserstein distance should yield a unique invariant law and geometric convergence from every initial distribution; deterministic fixed points then appear as Dirac-measure cases.

Coupled loops lead to product-state maps. Block Lipschitz estimates and spectral-radius conditions may provide consistency criteria even when individual channels do not contract independently, extending the theory from one feedback circuit to interacting causal networks.

\section{Conclusion}

A causal circuit is consistent when its one-circuit return map reproduces its boundary state. On a nonempty complete metric space, strict contraction guarantees exactly one such state, global convergence of all iterates, and the quantitative certificate
\[
d(x,x_\ast)\leq\frac{d(x,F(x))}{1-K}.
\]
Polynomial maps inherit these conclusions on complete invariant domains where they contract, and affine maps with $|a|<1$ have the explicit fixed point $b/(1-a)$. Finite strict contractions obey the same uniqueness principle.

The polynomial $x\mapsto x^2+1$ and Boolean negation show that neither a causal rule nor a polynomial causal rule guarantees consistency by itself. The mechanism converting Novikov's principle from a narrative demand into a mathematical consequence is stable feedback: each traversal shrinks disagreement until one closed history remains.

